 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Reflection Direction:** The method requires determining the direction of the reflection ray. If the point is not on a clear surface, the reflection direction may be ambiguous. The source notes do not specify how this is handled for non-surface points [S1].
-*   **Feature Representation:** The NeRF must be capable of outputting feature vectors that can be rendered and decoded into color. The source notes do not specify the dimensionality or nature of these features [S1].
+*   **Precision:** The spatial contraction function involves division by $(2 - \|\mathbf{x}\|)$, which approaches zero as $\|\mathbf{x}\|$ approaches 2. Care must be taken to avoid numerical instability for points near the boundary of the contracted sphere [S1].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The handling of the last sample in the depth range requires a defined $\Delta t$ [G].
+*   **Normal Estimation:** The method requires estimating surface normals from the NeRF density gradient. This can be noisy and may require smoothing or regularization [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method involves casting reflection rays for each sample on the primary ray, which increases the computational cost compared to standard NeRF. However, it is described as having "low computational overhead" compared to inverse rendering methods that use hundreds to thousands of sampled reflection rays [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF geometry and the small color decoder MLP. No additional large environment maps are needed, unlike Ref-NeRF [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Outperforms prior methods for view synthesis of scenes containing shiny objects and is the only existing NeRF method that can synthesize photorealistic specular appearance and reflections in real-world scenes while requiring comparable optimization time to current state-of-the-art view synthesis models [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently renders accurate reflections with low computational overhead; naturally synthesizes consistent reflections of nearby and distant content; avoids the need for large MLPs to encode the entire environment [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the NeRF geometry being accurate enough to support meaningful reflection ray tracing; performance may degrade in scenes with complex, non-Lambertian surfaces where the reflection direction is hard to estimate [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds the cost of casting and tracing reflection rays for each sample point along the camera ray. However, it avoids the expensive Monte Carlo integration over the entire hemisphere required by inverse rendering methods [S1]. The color decoder MLP is small, reducing the computational burden compared to large MLPs used in Ref-NeRF [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The method does not require storing the full environment map in the MLP, reducing memory usage compared to Ref-NeRF [S1].
+*   **Performance:** NeRF-Casting outperforms prior methods for view synthesis of scenes containing shiny objects and is the only existing NeRF method that can synthesize photorealistic specular appearance and reflections in real-world scenes while requiring comparable optimization time to current state-of-the-art view synthesis models [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient rendering of specular reflections, consistent reflections of nearby and distant content, lower computational overhead than inverse rendering methods.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the NeRF geometry for reflection ray tracing. Performance may degrade in scenes with complex lighting or transparent objects.
 
 ## Background
-NeRF-Casting was introduced in the paper "NeRF-Casting: Improved View-Dependent Appearance with Consistent Reflections" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of prior NeRF methods that rely on large, computationally expensive neural networks to model outgoing radiance, which limits optimization and rendering speed [S1]. The method builds on the standard NeRF volume rendering framework [S1].
